FatFs in ReactOS
================

This directory contains FatFs R0.16 with the upstream patch 2 applied.
It is the common FAT/exFAT implementation used by the image host tool,
FreeLdr, and the exFAT filesystem driver. Each consumer supplies only the
FatFs media-access and allocation callbacks appropriate to its environment.

Upstream: https://elm-chan.org/fsw/ff/
Archive:  https://elm-chan.org/fsw/ff/arc/ff16.zip
Archive SHA-256: 99f7dc1f7e095356e4a9e3dbe29959090d8b948afe2bbc5441e52fdf4b85449e
Release: R0.16, July 22, 2025

Applied upstream patches, in order:

1. https://elm-chan.org/fsw/ff/patch/ff16p1.diff
   SHA-256: 7996ddc3135f3d534a8153f7d75d587030e5c8551b003c3b7ee823ffb7fc64bf
2. https://elm-chan.org/fsw/ff/patch/ff16p2.diff
   SHA-256: 5cd39f1fc299f0f1dec9fa1ce544dc0bc048b2f6eb3b8161476ed20f9cf1e290

ReactOS changes are intentionally limited to:

* ffconf.h enables exFAT, UTF-8 long names, formatting, 64-bit LBA, and
  the media geometry needed by all three consumers. FF_LFN_UNICODE remains
  overridable so the kernel exFAT driver can use UTF-16 without a second copy
  of the configuration file.
* ff.h accepts FF_USE_REACTOS_TYPES so target builds use the SDK's fixed-width
  Windows types without including the user-mode Windows umbrella header.
* CMakeLists.txt defines the target-side static library.
* ff.c avoids two exFAT techniques covered by Microsoft patents that are
  still in force (see "Patent avoidance" below).

Patent avoidance
----------------

Two optional exFAT techniques are covered by Microsoft patents that, unlike
the core exFAT format patents (US8583708, expired January 2026), have not
yet expired. ff.c is locally modified to not practice them. Volumes written
this way remain fully spec-compliant and interoperate with Windows; both
features are still honored when reading volumes created by other systems.

1. Directory lookup by NameHash — US8321439 (est. expiry 2027-03-09) and
   US10614032 (est. expiry 2028-01-27), "Quick filename lookup using name
   hash". dir_find() no longer pre-filters candidates with the on-disk
   NameHash field; it compares name length and characters only. The
   NameHash field is still written on entry creation, as the specification
   requires and as chkdsk verifies.

2. NoFatChain (contiguous) allocation — US7873596 (est. expiry 2028-03-27),
   "Extending cluster allocations in an extensible file system". Cluster
   chains created or extended by this code are always recorded in the FAT
   and never marked NoFatChain in GeneralSecondaryFlags: create_chain()
   starts new chains FAT-linked and converts a contiguous chain to a FAT
   chain before extending it, remove_chain() no longer promotes chains back
   to 'contiguous', f_mkdir() and f_expand() write FAT chains for the
   clusters they allocate. Reading NoFatChain files and directories created
   by other implementations is unaffected, as is shrinking them in place.

The sites deviating from upstream are wrapped in __REACTOS__ preprocessor
blocks with the pristine upstream code in the #else branch, so a plain
build of these sources without __REACTOS__ behaves exactly like upstream.
Once the patents above have lapsed (verify against USPTO Patent Center,
the dates here are Google Patents estimates), the __REACTOS__ blocks can
simply be removed.

The media-access implementation is not kept in this directory. This preserves
the upstream separation between the filesystem core and platform I/O.
